{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789224229,"nonce":"hs9NjIEBuHHAMTve1o/r9I/XU0Y=","title":"Closure calls are never devirtualised: f.call(a, b) stays a dynamic dispatch","message":"bench/ash/closure_call.wren spends 150 ns per call on `f.call(sum, i)` while the equivalent method call inlines. The inline cache for a `call(_,_)` site on a closure records the receiver class (Fn) and the closure's function id, but devirtualisation guards on class only, which is meaningless for closures: every closure shares the Fn class. Guard on closure identity instead (compare the receiver's object pointer to the cached closure pointer, or the closure's function pointer for closures of the same function) and then take the same guarded known-call and inline paths methods take. Ash's closure_call runs at 1.5 ns per call through exactly that guard.","files":null}]}